item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;Yahoo Finance now charges $479/year to download historical data. Here are 7 free and paid alternatives, with prices, history depth and Python code.*&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Last updated: October 13, 2026 · Prices verified October 2026&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: some links below are affiliate links, and I may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. EODHD is a partner of mine. The prices, limits and cons in this article are my own reading of each provider's official pages.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quick answer:&lt;/strong&gt; Yahoo Finance historical data is still free to &lt;em&gt;view&lt;/em&gt;, but downloading it as CSV now requires Yahoo Finance Gold ($479.40/year). The cheapest reliable replacement is an end-of-day data API: &lt;strong&gt;EODHD&lt;/strong&gt; ($19.99/month, 30+ years, CSV or JSON) if you need many tickers or a script. &lt;strong&gt;Tiingo&lt;/strong&gt;'s free tier works for personal research, and &lt;strong&gt;Nasdaq.com&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;Stooq&lt;/strong&gt; cover a quick one-off download for free. If you only need a few prices, &lt;strong&gt;yfinance&lt;/strong&gt; still works, with rate limits and no guarantees.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;




&lt;p&gt;You click "Download" on Yahoo Finance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing downloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You get an upgrade screen instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For years, Yahoo Finance was the default answer to "where do I get historical stock data?" Analysts used it for Excel models. Students used it for theses. Developers used it as a free backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rebuilding a backtest that used to start with a Yahoo CSV,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;maintaining a spreadsheet that pulled prices every month,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;or running a Python script that suddenly returns empty DataFrames,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;this matters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What changed with Yahoo Finance historical data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yahoo's own help page is explicit: &lt;em&gt;"Downloading historical data is available with a Yahoo Finance Gold subscription."&lt;/em&gt; (&lt;a href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN2311.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Yahoo Help&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can still open any ticker, go to &lt;strong&gt;Historical Data&lt;/strong&gt;, pick a range and read the table in the browser. The &lt;strong&gt;Download&lt;/strong&gt; button is the part behind the paywall.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's what each Yahoo plan includes, from Yahoo's &lt;a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/about/plans/select-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;plan page&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Plan&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Price (billed yearly)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Historical data download&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No (view only)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bronze&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$95.40/year ($7.95/mo)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Silver&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$239.40/year ($19.95/mo)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Gold&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$479.40/year ($39.95/mo)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, CSV&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two more limits apply even with Gold. Yahoo says the feature &lt;em&gt;"isn't available for all instruments due to data licensing restrictions,"&lt;/em&gt; and the download is still one ticker at a time, by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paying $479 a year for a button is a lot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Especially when the button doesn't scale.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The real problem isn't the paywall
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most people searching for this want a CSV.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What they actually need is a &lt;strong&gt;repeatable source of clean daily prices&lt;/strong&gt;: same columns every time, adjusted for splits and dividends, available for hundreds of tickers, and not dependent on a website layout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A manual download from any website, free or paid, fails that test the moment you need more than a few tickers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the question isn't "how do I get the Yahoo button back?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's "what replaces it for my volume?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Yahoo Finance alternatives at a glance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Free tier&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Paid from&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;History depth&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Access&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Main catch&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://eodhd.com/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;EODHD&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20 calls/day, 1 year&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$19.99/mo ($199/yr)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30+ years&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API (CSV/JSON), Excel &amp;amp; Sheets add-ons&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free tier too small for real backtests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.tiingo.com/about/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tiingo&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,000 req/day, 500 symbols/mo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$30/mo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30+ years EOD&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;License is internal/personal use only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.alphavantage.co/premium/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Alpha Vantage&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25 req/day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$49.99/mo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Varies by endpoint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25 calls/day goes fast; some functions are premium&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://site.financialmodelingprep.com/developer/docs/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Financial Modeling Prep&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;250 calls/day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$19/mo (annual)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5 years on Basic and Starter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Short history on the cheaper plans&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/ranaroussi/yfinance" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;yfinance&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;n/a&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Whatever Yahoo serves&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Python library&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unofficial, rate-limited, personal use&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.nasdaq.com/market-activity/quotes/historical" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nasdaq.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;n/a&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up to 10 years&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Browser&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Manual, one ticker at a time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://stooq.com/db/h/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Stooq&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;n/a&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Long, varies by market&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Browser, bulk files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Automated downloads unreliable in 2026&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which type of replacement do you need?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There isn't one best Yahoo Finance alternative. There's a best one for your volume.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One ticker, once:&lt;/strong&gt; a free website (Nasdaq.com, Stooq) is enough.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A few tickers, regularly, personal project:&lt;/strong&gt; yfinance or a free API tier (Tiingo).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dozens to thousands of tickers, a backtest, or anything client-facing:&lt;/strong&gt; a paid end-of-day API with clear licensing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pick your row. Then read only the options that fit it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. EODHD: best overall Yahoo Finance replacement for historical data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;EODHD (EOD Historical Data) is an API built around exactly what the Yahoo download used to give you: daily open, high, low, close, adjusted close and volume. It covers 150,000+ tickers across global exchanges, with 30+ years of history on paid plans.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use it in most of my fintech tutorials, so I'll show the exact code I run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key features&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;End-of-day prices with an &lt;code&gt;adjusted_close&lt;/code&gt; field (adjusted for splits and dividends)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CSV or JSON output from the same endpoint (&lt;code&gt;fmt=csv&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;fmt=json&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Delisted tickers keep their history, which matters for backtests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Excel and Google Sheets add-ons for non-coders&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Replicate the Yahoo CSV in Python&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The script below returns the same columns as the old Yahoo download, in the same order. The &lt;code&gt;demo&lt;/code&gt; key works for AAPL.US, TSLA.US, AMZN.US and VTI.US, so you can run it before signing up.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;requests pandas
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pandas&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;API_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;EODHD_API_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;yahoo_style_history&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ticker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;DataFrame&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://eodhd.com/api/eod/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ticker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;api_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;API_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataFrame&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;columns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;high&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;High&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;low&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Low&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;adjusted_close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Adj Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;volume&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Volume&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;High&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Low&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Adj Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Volume&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;yahoo_style_history&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;AAPL.US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2026-10-05&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2026-10-09&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;to_csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;AAPL.csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;to_string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Output (real data, pulled October 13, 2026):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csvs"&gt;&lt;code&gt;      &lt;span class="k"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="k"&gt;Open&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="k"&gt;High&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="k"&gt;Low&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;Adj&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="k"&gt;Volume&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2026-10-05&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;332.82&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.21&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;331.65&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;332.89&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="mf"&gt;332.89&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;34400900&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2026-10-06&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;332.28&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;334.38&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;330.62&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;333.63&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="mf"&gt;333.63&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;30449000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2026-10-07&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.96&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;338.67&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;332.78&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.67&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.67&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;34147900&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2026-10-08&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.82&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;341.57&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;335.90&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;340.42&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="mf"&gt;340.42&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;35332400&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2026-10-09&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;331.70&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;338.61&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;330.70&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.64&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="mf"&gt;336.64&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;37881200&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Same columns. Same format. Your old Excel model or pandas code reads it without changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;History depth check&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same call with &lt;code&gt;start="1980-12-01"&lt;/code&gt; returns Apple's first trading day, December 12, 1980 (close 28.74, adjusted close 0.0981). The adjusted column already accounts for every split since then.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Delisted tickers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Twitter (TWTR.US) was delisted in October 2022. Its history is still there:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csvs"&gt;&lt;code&gt;      &lt;span class="k"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="k"&gt;volume&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2022-10-24&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;51.52&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;24296045&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2022-10-25&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;52.78&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;37489316&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2022-10-26&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;53.35&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;29698456&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="ld"&gt;2022-10-27&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="mf"&gt;53.70&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;198415408&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If your backtest only uses companies that still exist, it's biased toward the winners. Keeping delisted tickers fixes that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Download many tickers at once&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tickers&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;AAPL.US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;TSLA.US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;AMZN.US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;VTI.US&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tickers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;yahoo_style_history&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2016-01-01&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2026-10-09&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;to_csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;.csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Swap the list for your own universe once you're on a paid key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pricing (verified October 2026)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Free: 20 API calls/day, 1 year of history&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;EOD Historical All-World: $19.99/month or $199/year, 100,000 calls/day, 30+ years&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;EOD + Intraday: $29.99/month&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's $199 a year against $479.40 for Yahoo Gold: about 58% less. And you get an API instead of a button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://eodhd.com/?via=kmg&amp;amp;ref1=Meneses&amp;amp;utm_source=medium&amp;amp;utm_medium=post&amp;amp;utm_campaign=yahoo-finance-historical-data-download-alternatives&amp;amp;utm_content=Meneses" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try EODHD here&lt;/a&gt;. Code &lt;strong&gt;KEVIN10&lt;/strong&gt; gives 10% off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The CSV/JSON switch is a single parameter.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Delisted tickers and full split/dividend adjustment out of the box.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Annual plan is cheaper than any Yahoo tier that includes downloads.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The free tier (20 calls/day, 1 year) is for testing, not for real research.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;adjusted_close&lt;/code&gt; is recalculated when new dividends arrive, so don't use it as a fixed key in a database.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fundamentals are a separate, more expensive plan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; anyone replacing Yahoo downloads at scale, from Excel analysts to Python backtests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; you need fewer than five tickers, once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Tiingo: best free tier for personal research
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tiingo gives you 30+ years of end-of-day prices on its free Starter plan, which is generous by any standard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pricing (verified October 2026)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Free: 50 requests/hour, 1,000/day, 500 unique symbols/month&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Power: $30/month or $300/year for individuals&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deep history on the free plan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;500 symbols a month covers most personal portfolios.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Both tiers are licensed for &lt;em&gt;"Internal Use Only"&lt;/em&gt;: no displaying or sharing the data. Internal commercial use costs $50/month, and redistribution is priced separately.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;50 requests per hour slows down bulk downloads.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; personal backtests and study projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; you're building something other people will see.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Alpha Vantage: best known free API, smallest daily quota
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Alpha Vantage is often the first API developers try. It covers time series, technical indicators, fundamentals and FX.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pricing (verified October 2026)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Free: 25 API requests/day&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Premium: from $49.99/month (75 requests/minute)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Broad catalog beyond prices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plenty of community examples.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;25 requests a day means 25 tickers a day, at best.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Some functions are premium-only, so check each endpoint before you build on it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; small experiments and learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; you need more than a handful of tickers per day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I compared Alpha Vantage with six other providers in &lt;a href="https://medium.com/coinmonks/the-7-best-financial-apis-for-investors-and-developers-in-2025-in-depth-analysis-and-comparison-adbc22024f68" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;this comparison of financial APIs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Financial Modeling Prep: generous free calls, shorter history
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FMP has a large API catalog, especially for fundamentals and financial statements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pricing (verified October 2026)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Basic (free): 250 calls/day, 5 years of EOD history&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Starter: $19/month billed annually, also 5 years&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;250 free calls a day is ten times Alpha Vantage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong fundamentals coverage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;5 years of history on the cheaper plans. Long-horizon backtests need a higher tier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; fundamentals-heavy projects with recent price history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; you need decades of prices on a small budget.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. yfinance: free, until it isn't
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;yfinance is a Python library that reads Yahoo's data. Many people still use it, and for a few tickers it often works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But know what you're relying on. The project's own README says it is &lt;em&gt;"not affiliated, endorsed, or vetted by Yahoo, Inc."&lt;/em&gt; and that the data is &lt;em&gt;"intended for personal use only."&lt;/em&gt; (&lt;a href="https://github.com/ranaroussi/yfinance" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In 2025, users reported &lt;code&gt;YFRateLimitError('Too Many Requests. Rate limited. Try after a while.')&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;a href="https://github.com/ranaroussi/yfinance/issues/2422" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;issue #2422&lt;/a&gt;) and failed historical requests that they linked to the new paywall (&lt;a href="https://github.com/ranaroussi/yfinance/issues/2340" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;issue #2340&lt;/a&gt;). Fixes usually meant upgrading the library or changing how requests are sent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Free and two lines of code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Same data you saw on the Yahoo website.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No SLA. A Yahoo change can break your pipeline overnight.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rate limits hit hardest when you loop over many tickers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Personal use only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; quick, personal checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; anything depends on the data arriving tomorrow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Nasdaq.com: free and manual
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nasdaq's &lt;a href="https://www.nasdaq.com/market-activity/quotes/historical" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;historical quotes page&lt;/a&gt; shows &lt;em&gt;"up to 10 years of daily historical stock prices and volumes for each stock."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Free, official exchange source.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Enough history for most one-off charts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10 years maximum.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Manual, one ticker at a time. No API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; a single chart or a quick sanity check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; you need more than 10 years, or more than a few tickers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Stooq: free bulk files, fragile automation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stooq offers free historical files for many markets, including bulk daily packages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I liked&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Free bulk downloads, no account needed for manual use.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long histories for many markets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it falls short&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automated access got shaky in 2026. Users of portfolio software reported empty responses from Stooq's historical servers starting in April 2026, while manual CSV downloads still worked (&lt;a href="https://fundmanagersoftware.com/forum/viewtopic.php?p=28752" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;forum thread&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Licensing terms for reuse aren't clear, so treat it as personal research data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Best for:&lt;/strong&gt; a free bulk snapshot for personal research.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skip if:&lt;/strong&gt; your workflow depends on scheduled downloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Best Yahoo Finance alternative by use case
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Use case&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pick&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Excel model with monthly price updates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;EODHD (Excel add-on)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same columns as Yahoo, no manual downloads&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Backtest over 10+ years and hundreds of tickers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;EODHD&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30+ years, delisted tickers, 100k calls/day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Personal research, under 500 symbols&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tiingo free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30+ years at $0, personal-use license&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fundamentals plus recent prices&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Financial Modeling Prep&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Large fundamentals catalog, 250 free calls&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One chart, one time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nasdaq.com&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free, 10 years, no signup&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Quick Python check on a few tickers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yfinance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free, but expect rate limits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Can you still get Yahoo Finance historical data for free?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Partly. You can view the full historical table on any quote page and copy it into a spreadsheet by hand. For long ranges that means scrolling through thousands of rows, and Yahoo's data is meant for personal use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It works for one ticker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't work as a system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're building anything bigger, I covered the full workflow for pulling and storing price history in &lt;a href="https://medium.com/codex/api-for-historical-stock-data-a-practical-guide-for-developers-140dc3b9f802" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API for Historical Stock Data: A Practical Guide&lt;/a&gt;. For options data, see &lt;a href="https://medium.com/codex/i-pulled-eodhds-full-options-history-with-python-here-s-what-i-learned-8e19fbe8a951" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;I Pulled EODHD's Full Options History With Python&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is Yahoo Finance historical data free?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Viewing it is free. Downloading it as CSV requires Yahoo Finance Gold, which costs $479.40 per year billed annually.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why can't I download historical data from Yahoo Finance?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yahoo moved the Download button to the Gold plan. Some instruments can't be downloaded even with Gold because of data licensing restrictions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How much is Yahoo Finance Gold?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
$39.95 per month billed yearly, or $479.40 per year (October 2026). Bronze and Silver don't include downloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does yfinance still work in 2026?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Often, yes, for small volumes. It's unofficial, intended for personal use, and has had rate-limit errors (&lt;code&gt;YFRateLimitError&lt;/code&gt;) since 2025.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the best free alternative to Yahoo Finance for historical data?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Tiingo's free plan offers 30+ years of daily prices for up to 500 symbols a month, for personal use. For a single quick download, Nasdaq.com gives up to 10 years.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the cheapest paid alternative to Yahoo Finance Gold for downloads?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
EODHD's end-of-day plan costs $199 per year (or $19.99/month) for 30+ years of global data via API, CSV, Excel or Google Sheets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can I use these alternatives in Excel or Google Sheets?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yes. EODHD offers Excel and Google Sheets add-ons, and every API above returns CSV or JSON that Excel can import.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Verdict
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yahoo didn't remove historical data. It removed the free button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a one-off, use Nasdaq.com. For personal research, use Tiingo's free tier. For anything you'll run every week, or show to someone else, use an end-of-day API like EODHD at less than half the price of Yahoo Gold.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://eodhd.com/?via=kmg&amp;amp;ref1=Meneses&amp;amp;utm_source=medium&amp;amp;utm_medium=post&amp;amp;utm_campaign=yahoo-finance-historical-data-download-alternatives&amp;amp;utm_content=Meneses" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Get EODHD here&lt;/a&gt; and use &lt;strong&gt;KEVIN10&lt;/strong&gt; for 10% off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The data was never the hard part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Getting it the same way every time is.&lt;/p&gt;




